ind of do the  same operations on them separately and  which is cool Grail is another language  from this area and also from this era it  also ran on the ran tablet so this is  the first example of a flow chart based  programming language you'd actually draw  those different sections of the program  and draw lines to connect them up and it  would even use an OCR like technology to  turn the drawings and letters you drew  into into program components into the  names of things which is kind of  fascinating when you think about it like  we've been working on OCR for over 50  years now and it's still only work some  of the time another one I thought was  really interesting was Pygmalion which  the creator had this thesis that part of  the complexity of programming was  holding all of the mental model of your  program state in your head and so he  wanted to be able to show all the state  on-screen which is what you see in those  boxes so the idea was every piece of  state your program you could just sort  of look at it all at once it also uses  this program programming by example  paradigm so you would kind of show the  program what to do with your data I  showed each step in this remember mode  and then you could use that to output  like a program that you could take with  you and run against an arbitrary input  later another favorite one of mine this  is cube from 1995 this is I've been told  the very first 3d visual programming  which I have no idea how this works like  galaxy brain right I just looked at this  picture and I was like I have to put  this on the slide so this is all very  fun but what's the catch there are some  things that are very hard to do in  visual languages  abstraction is really hard you probably  have to create a new paradigm to do that  difficut merging are really hard like if  you made this beautiful visual thing and  I made my visual thing like how do we  determine what's different and merge  them together it's really hard to show  that and 